<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Barbatos.SecsGem.Substrates</PackageId>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(BarbatosTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
    <RootNamespace>Barbatos.SecsGem.Substrates</RootNamespace>
    <PackageTags>secs;e90;substrate;wafer;tracking;fab300;e87;stream14</PackageTags>
    <Description>Substrate tracking (SEMI E90) for .NET: the substrate and substrate location objects, the state machines E90 gives them, and the rules about which transition may follow which - enforced, with a refusal that names the state that refused it. E90 has no message set of its own: a substrate is an object, and its attributes reach a host through SECS-II stream 14 object services, which is what this package exposes. Depends on Barbatos.SecsGem.Objects and Barbatos.SecsGem.Hsms, and not on GEM or E87: the collection events E90 defines are raised as events for an application to publish through whatever reports it already has, and a machine that tracks substrates without carriers can use this on its own.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Barbatos.SecsGem.Core\Barbatos.SecsGem.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Barbatos.SecsGem.Hsms\Barbatos.SecsGem.Hsms.csproj" />
    <!-- E90 is an object model and nothing else: every attribute a host reads comes through
         stream 14, so this is the one dependency that is not optional. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Barbatos.SecsGem.Objects\Barbatos.SecsGem.Objects.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
